# Pages

Pages are rendered on the server from Howdy UI components (`howdy/ui`),
which are Lustre elements. The full component list, with signatures, is in
[components.md](components.md); check a component's arguments there before
using it, since they vary.

## A page

```gleam
import howdy/controller.{type GuardedContext}
import howdy/ui
import howdy/ui/button
import lustre/element.{text}
import {{.Name}}/layouts/default_layout

pub fn show(ctx: GuardedContext(guarded), notes: List(Note)) {
  default_layout.render(ctx, "Notes", [
    ui.h1("Notes"),
    ui.p([text("Everything you wrote down.")]),
    ui.stack([], list.map(notes, note_card)),
  ])
}
```

- Start new pages with `howdy create page <feature> <page>`: it writes the
  page, its route and a test. Pass data as extra parameters and call it
  from the route: `controller.get("/", fn(ctx) { index_page.show(ctx, items) })`.
- The layout (`src/{{.Name}}/layouts/`) supplies the document, theme,
  stylesheets and header; `render(ctx, title, content)` sets the `<title>`,
  and the page adds its own `ui.h1`. `howdy create layout <name>` makes
  another.
- Set a status with `|> controller.with_status(404)`.
- `text(...)` and attribute values are escaped. Never build HTML strings.

## Components: what trips people up

- Arities differ. `ui.h1("Title")` to `ui.h4` and `ui.muted("...")` take a
  String. `ui.p(children)`, `ui.link(href, children)`, `ui.card_title(children)`
  take no attributes. Most others are `(attributes, children)`, some with a
  variant first: `ui.button(button.Primary, [], [text("Save")])`.
- Variants live in the component's module: `import howdy/ui/button`, then
  `button.Primary | Secondary | Outline | Ghost | Link | Danger`.
- `ui.button` is always `type="button"` and never submits. Use
  `ui.submit_button(button.Primary, [], [text("Save")])` in a form.
- Dialog, popover, menu and tooltip triggers return a list of attributes:
  `ui.button(button.Outline, ui.dialog_trigger("rename"), [text("Rename")])`.
- Mix in plain Lustre freely (`lustre/element/html as h`,
  `lustre/attribute as a`, `lustre/event`) for anything without a component.

## Forms

Plain HTML forms that work without JavaScript. Once `howdy create pages`
has run, `src/{{.Name}}/platform/pages.gleam` has the helpers
(`read_form`, `field`, `checkbox`, `submit`, `redirect`, `action`,
`error`); use them, and look at the generated `pages/` of that feature as
the model. Without it, build the form from `ui.label`, `ui.input`,
`ui.textarea`, `ui.checkbox`, `ui.native_select` and `ui.field_error`, and
read it with `howdy/form`.

The flow, from a generated controller:

```gleam
|> controller.post("/", fn(ctx) {
  use form <- pages.read_form(ctx)
  case helpers.parse(form) {
    Error(errors) ->
      new_page.show(ctx, form, errors) |> controller.with_status(422)
    Ok(input) ->
      case notes.create(db, input) {
        Ok(item) -> pages.redirect(ctx, to: helpers.path(item))  // 303
        Error(error) -> pages.error(ctx, error)
      }
  }
})
```

- On failure, render the same form again with 422, the submitted values
  and the errors, which `pages.field` shows under each field.
- On success, redirect (303) so a refresh does not post again.
- Forms can only GET and POST: update is `POST /notes/:id`, delete is
  `POST /notes/:id/delete` (`pages.action(button.Danger, to: path, label: "Delete")`).
  Never change anything on GET.
- Put `/new` before `/:id` in the controller.

## Live pages and components

A live view is a Lustre server component: its model, update and view run
on the server, and the browser shows each change over a WebSocket. Use one
for things that change while the page is open; use a plain page and a form
otherwise.

- `howdy create live_page <feature> <page>` or
  `howdy create live_component [feature] <name>` writes it and routes its
  socket. `src/{{.Name}}/features/home/components/` has a worked example
  if the project was made with a database.
- A page that shows one must render with `render_live`, not `render`, and
  include `<name>_component.mount()` (or `live.mount("/socket/url")`).
- Its app is `lustre.component(init, update, view, options)`. Pass the
  database in by closing over it (`pub fn app(db: Repo)`), call the
  feature's operations module from `update`, and keep errors in the model.
- Events come from `lustre/event`: `event.on_click(Msg)`,
  `event.on_input(fn(value) { Typed(value) })`, `event.on_check`,
  `event.on_submit(fn(fields) { Submit })` (which prevents the default).
- State lives per connection: it is lost on reload, so save anything that
  matters to the database.
- Tests cannot open the socket (it answers 426): test `update` and `view`
  as plain functions.

## Styling

- Howdy UI's styles come from the theme. `priv/public/assets/ui.css` is
  generated: run `gleam run -m tasks/css` after changing themes, and never
  edit it by hand. Under `gleam dev` it is generated on request.
- Your own CSS goes in `priv/public/assets/style.css`, loaded after it,
  with plain kebab-case classes set by `a.class("note-card")`. Howdy UI's
  own class names are hashed; do not target them.
- Prefer layout components (`ui.container`, `ui.stack`, `ui.row`,
  `ui.card`) over new CSS.
- Themes: `howdy/ui/theme` and the presets in `howdy/ui/themes/`
  (`zinc`, `stone`, `rose`, `green`, `violet`). A theme change must be made
  in the layout, `src/tasks/css.gleam` and the dev stylesheet route
  together.

## Check

`howdy check`, then look at the page under `gleam dev` at
http://127.0.0.1:8787. Test pages by status and by text on the page (see
the howdy-testing skill).
